// The boss journal: Azerath the Sunken Archmage, Master of the Seven Circles, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).archmage = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A tower gone under the sea, seven circles cut in the floor, and a wizard who never once sets foot on them. Two of us wore runes joined by a thread, and the next spell told them to close ranks or part. Wet boots, wrong guess. Gave ground.",
    gloomsworn: "A tower sunk to its windows, and a master who stayed at his desk while the water rose. I know a drowned place when I stand in one. Two of us were tied by a bright thread and told to close or part. I guessed wrong, quietly.",
    thresher: "He keeps it like a salon, this one. Four kinds of weather in his floor and a wizard hovering over them like a chandelier. Two of us were tied with a thread and told to bunch or scatter. I scattered. It was the wrong answer.",
    bloodreaver: "A floating old man with a staff and a drowned hall! I liked him at once. Then two of us wore runes and a thread, and the right answer was to stand close or far, and nobody told me which. I got burned for guessing. A good burn. I will guess better.",
    runeguard: "Seven circles carved into the floor, and a master who reads them as I read omens. Two of us bore runes and a thread between; the name of the next spell decided close or apart. I had not looked at the name. I will always look at the name now.",
    gorgemaw: "A sunken house with the kitchen gone to the fishes and the host floating over his own table, never sitting. Two guests tied with a thread and told to share a plate or not. I ate the wrong way. Cold soup, hot floor.",
    oathbroker: "The Archmage holds a sunken tower under seven circles of very old law. When two of the undersigned are bound by runes, the next spell on his docket sets the terms: together or apart. I did not read the docket. Breach noted.",
    ironpalm: "He does not move. He floats, and the floor speaks for him: circles, colours, runes tied by a thread. I listened to the wrong word and stood where the right one would have been. Burned. The floor taught me the sentence afterwards.",
    ranger: "Wizard doesn't walk. Floats in the middle, lets the floor do the work. Two of us got runes and a thread between. Next spell says close up or spread out. Guessed. Wrong. Learn the order, then shoot.",
    shadowblade: "A floating mark who never turns his back, because he has no back worth the name: he just rotates. Two of us were tied with a thread and told to close or part. I chose to part, naturally. Not that time, darling.",
    pyromancer: "A wizard who commands FOUR elements, and one of them is mine! The fire rings were delicious. The frost I found insulting. Two of us wore runes and a thread, and I stood close when I should have stood far. Singed in good company.",
    thermist: "Four elements on one floor and a man floating in the middle conducting them! Hot, cold, bolt, void, and I was thrilled until two of us were tied with a thread and had to guess: together or apart. Wrong. Result: instructive.",
    driftblade: "He floats in the middle and never moves and the whole floor moves for him, and two of us wore runes and a thread and had to choose, together or apart, and I chose, and I ran through the wrong circle and was singed to the elbow.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man never leaves centre stage; he floats there and lets the set do the acting. Seven circles, four coloured lamps, and two of the company tied by a thread, told by the next cue to close or part. Cue missed. (Not by the Doll.)",
    cleric: "Poor Azerath, still at his desk under the sea, still working. Two of my flock wore runes on a bright thread, and the next spell said close or part, and I did not know which, and one of them paid for my not knowing. Forgive me. I read the list now.",
    grovekeeper: "Oh, he's been in that tower a long time, dear; he's gone quite damp. Four elements in a ring, like the four winds. Two of my lot got tied with a thread and had to bunch or scatter. I picked the wrong wind. Never mind. I will read it next time.",
    chronomancer: "A man floating in the middle of a drowned tower, casting in tempos I am almost sure I have heard before. Two of us wore runes and a thread and had to close or part. I will have chosen right by now. I did not. Not yet.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one archmage, sunken, still working. A colleague, in a sense, and I covet his notes. Ailments: four elements in rotation, two runes tied by a thread. Note: the name of the spell to come tells the pair to close or part. I did not check the name.",
    threadweaver: "A wizard tying two of my stitches together with a bright thread of his own, without so much as asking. The nerve. The next spell decided whether to pull it tight or let it out, and I guessed. Wrong. A sloppy seam, and his, but my fault.",
    tidecaller: "A tower drowned to its windows, and I felt at home, for the sea had been here first. Two of us wore runes on a bright thread, and the name of the spell to follow said whether to close or part. I guessed wrong once, and then I learned to read.",
    lanternkeeper: "A tower drowned to its windows, and two of us wore runes on a bright thread while the rest waited to be told why. I guessed wrong once, and my lantern burned in the wrong half of the room. Tomorrow I read the spell before I light. Keep your light.",
    kenseier: "A tower drowned in the sea, seven circles cut in the floor, and a wizard who never steps on them. Two of us wore runes joined by a thread, and the next spell named whether to close or part. I read it late. His staff, at least, keeps an honest count.",
    houndmaster: "Drowned tower, circles cut in the floor, and a wizard who never steps on them. Two of us got runes with a thread between, and the dogs couldn't tell friend from spell. Guessed wrong on the order. Learn the order, then hunt.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The Archmage is down and the circles have gone dark. We held the floor through four tempers of his, and the last where all of them lit at once. Boots wet to the knee. I would do it again, Sergeant.",
    gloomsworn: "The Archmage is down, and the circles have gone dark under the water. He kept his watch long after the sea came. So did we, and ours ended better. I stood a moment in the quiet of his tower. I have slept in worse.",
    thresher: "He is down, and the circles went out one after another like candles at the end of a ball. A tidy man, for a wizard. The floor will take back the tower in time. I would have liked one more waltz on it.",
    bloodreaver: "The old man is down! Every circle lit at the end and I took a burn from each, red, blue, yellow and violet, and loved every colour. A fine scar. A great tower, a better fight. Thank you, my friend.",
    runeguard: "Azerath is finished. The circles are dark, and every rune I could not read at first I read in time at the last. As foreseen, finally. I copied three of his sigils onto my cuff when no one was looking.",
    gorgemaw: "The host of the sunken house is down, and the last course came all at once, every burner lit. It was too much, and I loved it. The sea may have the kitchen now. I am full of ideas and not at all full.",
    oathbroker: "The Archmage's tenancy of the tower is ended, and the seven circles revert to the sea. Every term on his docket was met, by every one of the undersigned. Countersigned, with a flourish.",
    ironpalm: "The last circle went dark. He had said all that he had to say, and we had answered every word. Water ran over the floor and I stood in it, and breathed.",
    ranger: "Wizard's down. Floor gone quiet. Learned the order of his spells the way you learn a river. Best shot of the night was the last. Didn't tell anyone.",
    shadowblade: "The old man finally sank with his tower, as is proper. The circles went out one by one and the dark came back to me like a cat. He never saw me. He was too busy looking at the floor.",
    pyromancer: "I outshone the Archmage! Four elements and the fire was the best of them, and everyone agrees. All my sleeves intact, mostly. I shall describe the Pyre Rings at dinner for years.",
    thermist: "He's done! Hot, cold, bolt and void, all of them, and I solved the sequence, mostly! I stood in the dark afterward feeling like a clean slate. Result: elegant. I need to write this down while I am still warm.",
    driftblade: "He fell and the circles went dark and the whole tower sighed and I ran out through the water and up the stairs two at a time and never once slowed down, not even for the last spell.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for the master of the house. The company hit every cue in the last act, every lamp lit at once, and the Doll took its bow on the centre circle. (Ovation, from the sea.)",
    cleric: "The Archmage is at rest, and I think it is the first rest he has had in a hundred years. The circles went dark gently. Everyone is breathing, and wet, and very cross with me for fussing. Dawn keep him.",
    grovekeeper: "The old man's gone to his rest, dear, and the sea will keep the tower as a reef keeps a wreck. I thought it quite beautiful, in the end. My lot came out limping and dripping and quite cheerful.",
    chronomancer: "He fell. I knew he would, somewhere in my second attempt, or my third. The circles went out together, which was just as I had remembered. Nobody had to be undone at the end, or only the once.",
    alembist: "Result: one archmage, retired. Shelves bare, every patient alive. His notes are waterlogged and fascinating, and I have taken two pages for study. Label: do not read aloud. I have already read aloud.",
    threadweaver: "He's down, and the thread he tied me with came loose of its own accord, as any good knot does. Every one of my stitches came out dry-ish and in one piece. The seams held. I'll darn the tower's curtains if it asks.",
    tidecaller: "The circles went dark and the water in the tower stood still, slack at last. He had four moods and a fifth where all of them lit together, and we came through each. I stayed a while in his quiet. Mind the tide.",
    lanternkeeper: "The circles went dark one by one and the tower stood quiet. He had four moods and a fifth where all of them lit at once, and we came through every one. I left a lantern burning on the bottom stair. Walk where it's warm.",
    kenseier: "The Archmage is down and the circles are dark. Each of his tempers kept time differently, and by the last I could hear them all. I wiped the blade and the water ran off it. Ash settles.",
    houndmaster: "Wizard's down and the circles have gone dark. Learnt his spells the way you learn a poacher's rounds. Rust sniffed the staff and sneezed. Good dogs. We had him by supper.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Arcane Burst": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Raw light across the whole room, and no shield for it. Mend first, then stand: the drill. I set my feet, came to it whole, and kept the line tight on the healer.",
      gloomsworn: "The burst took all of us. There was no corner to stand in, and the dark had no hand in it. I came to it whole, and let it go through me like a wave through a drowned door. Wet, mostly.",
      thresher: "It lands on all of us across the whole floor. No shelter. Best answer is to be full of mending beforehand, a glass poured to the brim, and swing on through it. Light, that stuff.",
      bloodreaver: "Raw light across the whole room, and nowhere to put my shield! It tickled me and hurt the others, which seems unfair. So we came to it topped up, and stood close to the healer. My friend, you can do better.",
      runeguard: "The Burst reaches every one of us and cannot be stepped from, warded, or read in time to flee. The only answer is to arrive whole. A plain entry in the margin. Some omens only tell you to prepare.",
      gorgemaw: "A course for the whole table, served at once; no one can eat it for another. We sat down to it well fed, swallowed, and asked for mending after. Thin, but filling.",
      oathbroker: "A flat levy, due from every one of the undersigned at once. No clause excuses any party. The sole provision is to arrive in good standing, and we did. Entered without remark.",
      ironpalm: "Light, for all of us, at once. There is nowhere to stand against it. We came to it whole. It passed, as light passes.",
      ranger: "Burst. Everyone, no cover. Came to it healed. Back to the shot.",
      shadowblade: "A blast of light with no corner to hide in, which seems an insult to my profession. We came to it whole and took it together. Light has no manners.",
      pyromancer: "He lit the room like a bonfire and nobody could look away, which I admired, and nobody could step out, which I resented. We were topped up before it came and I kept casting straight through. Not my best light, though.",
      thermist: "Burst. Light, heat, everything at once, no way out. Be whole before it. Then it hit and I felt it all the way down to the marrow. Whole? Yes. Alive? Delightfully.",
      driftblade: "Light went through the whole room and I couldn't run from it, which I take personally, so I met it whole and went right on through the glare and out the far side without slowing.",
      marionettist: "A flash that lit the entire house, stalls to gods, and no exit. The company came to it whole and played on through the glare. (The Doll blinked. We pretended not to see.)",
      cleric: "The light fell on every one of us at once. I had the mending laid before it came, as I try to, and they came through it blinking and cross. Better cross than carried.",
      grovekeeper: "It comes like a flash of sun after a long rain, dear, over everyone. I had my lot planted with mending already, and the sprouts held. Be full before the light, and nobody goes in the ditch.",
      chronomancer: "The burst lands a beat after his hands part. I had everyone mended just before, which was the moment I'd chosen for it last time, or next time. I forget which. It worked.",
      alembist: "Observation: the Burst, felt by all, nothing to step from. Dose everyone before it comes and keep the big flask uncorked for after. Front shelf. Do not shake.",
      threadweaver: "The Burst ran along every thread at once, and there's no tying that off. I made sure the whole hem was whole before it came, and pulled the slack tight after. No snags.",
      tidecaller: "A flash across the whole room, and no corner of it was shaded. A blow like that is only weather, so I had let the water climb beforehand, and each of us came to it full and left it standing.",
      lanternkeeper: "A flash across the whole room, with no corner shaded. I had the lanterns lit and the party close, and let them all burst as the light gathered. Everyone met it already mended, and left it standing.",
      kenseier: "Raw light across the whole room, and no corner of it shaded. I stood close to the healer, came to it whole, and took it. A blow with no beat is one a blade cannot meet.",
      houndmaster: "Raw light across the whole room, no corner shaded. Stood close by the healer, dogs at my heels, came to it whole, and took it. Nothing to do but be ready.",
    } },
    "Arcane Lance": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Lance was meant for me. A wide sweep in front of him; I watched his staff level and had the shield up in the last instant. The line stayed out of the arc. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The Lance was meant for whoever stands at his front, and that was me. I drew the dark about me and took it whole, and the others kept out of the arc. A cold thing, a lance. Not the coldest.",
      thresher: "A long wide sweep in front of him, aimed at our knight, a blade the width of a door. I kept to his flank and out of the arc. Anyone in front of him is cut down where they stand.",
      bloodreaver: "The Lance! Right through me, a wide bright sweep in front of him, and it rattled my teeth. A good one. I took it, hit back while the ache was fresh, and the others kept clear. Cheers, old man.",
      runeguard: "His staff levelled and the arc before him was drawn upon the floor. A ward upon whoever stood in it, a breath before, and everyone else outside the sweep. It is the only blow of his that announces its victim by position.",
      gorgemaw: "The Lance is served to me alone, a broad sweep across the front of the table. I opened wide just before it and kept my friends off my plate. Sharp, with a long finish.",
      oathbroker: "The Lance is payable by whoever stands before him. I took delivery in full, behind every protection I carried. The fine print: the sweep is wide, but only in front. The undersigned stood to his sides. Surety given.",
      ironpalm: "He lowered the staff and the arc came wide before him. I met it alone. The others stood aside. I took half of it, and answered.",
      ranger: "Wide sweep in front of him, meant for our knight. Stay to his flank. Anyone in the arc gets cut.",
      shadowblade: "The old man swept his staff across the front of the room, and I watched from the side, where such blades do not reach. Rude of him to aim at someone else. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A bright wide beam from the old man's staff, aimed at our knight! I stood off to the side and watched it go by with some envy; I do appreciate a good beam. Out of the arc. Not singed.",
      thermist: "Lance. Bright, wide, in front of him, at one of us. Stay out of the arc. Our knight takes it, the rest of us on the flanks, striking. A very tidy division of labour.",
      driftblade: "The Lance came across the front of him in a great bright sweep and I was already round his side and through the gap behind the staff, and our knight took it, and I went out the far flank without breaking stride.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man levelled his staff and swept the front of the stage, aimed at the knight who plays opposite him. The company kept to the wings. (The Doll applauded from a very safe distance.)",
      cleric: "He swept the front of the room with that long bright lance and our knight stood in it, as he must. I laid the light on him before it landed and asked the rest of my flock to stand to the side. Bless him.",
      grovekeeper: "A long bright sweep in front of the old man, dear, aimed at our knight. I had my planting in him already, so the mending was growing when it struck. My lot kept off to the sides, out of the way of the plough.",
      chronomancer: "The lance falls a beat after his staff lowers. I had our knight warded in the moment before, which I will have done. The rest of us stood at his sides, outside the arc, where I have always stood.",
      alembist: "Label it plainly: do not stand in front of the staff. One heavy sweep, front of him only, at whoever holds him. A guard on our shield, and everyone else out of the arc. I stood at the side, clutching my satchel.",
      threadweaver: "The Lance landed on our knight, wide across the front of him, and I ran a little of it down a thread so he did not take all of it. Every other stitch at his flanks. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "A long bright sweep in front of him, meant for our knight. I watched the staff level and sent the water to him before it fell, while the rest of us kept to the flanks, out of the arc, like boats off a bar.",
      lanternkeeper: "A long bright lance swept the front of the room, meant for our knight. I hung a lantern on him before it fell, and the rest of us stood out of the arc, at the flanks.",
      kenseier: "The Lance was meant for me, a wide sweep before him. I watched his staff level and met it with the blade as it landed. The others stayed out of the arc. The staff always levels first, which is a courtesy.",
      houndmaster: "Wide sweep in front of him, meant for the shield-man. Kept to his flank, dogs too. Anyone in the arc gets cut. His staff levels first, so you see it coming.",
    } },
    "Rune Mark": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of us wore runes and a thread between. The name of the next spell said close ranks or fall apart. I read his list and called it, once I learned to look. Read orders before moving.",
      gloomsworn: "A bright thread tied two of us together, a rune each. The name of the spell that followed said whether to close or part. A thread like that is a leash for the living. I read the list before anyone moved.",
      thresher: "Two of us got a rune each and a thread between, a leash for two. The next spell on the list says whether to bunch or scatter. Read the list first. Moving before you have read it is dancing in the dark.",
      bloodreaver: "Two of us got runes and a bright thread, linked together! The next spell on his list says close or far, and I stopped guessing and started reading. It cost me a few burns to learn. I would pay them again.",
      runeguard: "Two runes, two elements, a thread between the bearers; the name of the spell that follows tells them close or apart. I read the upcoming list at once, as one ought. A pleasure to find a mage who labels his work.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests paired with a thread and a little rune each. The next dish on the menu tells them whether to share a plate or sit at opposite ends. I read the menu now.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties bound by rune, a thread between them. The spell next on the docket sets the terms: together or apart. I now read the docket before anything else. A man should always read before he signs.",
      ironpalm: "Two of us were tied by a thread, a rune apiece. I read the next spell on the list: close, or apart. The thread was the question and the list was the answer.",
      ranger: "Runes on two of us, thread between. Check the next spell on his list. Says close up or fan out. Then move.",
      shadowblade: "Two of us were tied by a thread, which is vulgar. The next spell's name told them to close or part, and I read it before I moved, because I am not a fool. Reading is half the art.",
      pyromancer: "Two of us wore runes joined by a thread, like a glittering necklace! The name of the next spell told them to bunch up or fan out. I looked at the name this time, which I did not before. Lesson learned. Hair intact.",
      thermist: "Runes on two of us, a thread between, two different elements! The next spell's name says close or apart. I read it too late last time and hot and cold fought over my eyebrows. Reading first. Always.",
      driftblade: "Two of us got runes and a thread and the next spell said whether to bunch or scatter, and I read it, finally, before I ran, so I knew where to run and how far and whether to take the other with me.",
      marionettist: "Two of the company were tied by a thread, a rune apiece, like a hastily rigged duet. The next cue told them to close or part, and now I read the cue before the curtain. (Always read the cue.)",
      cleric: "Two of my flock wore runes on a bright thread, and the name of the spell to come said close or part. I called it out for them, gently, the moment it appeared. They went the right way. Dawn keep them.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot got a rune apiece and a thread between them, dear, like two seedlings tied to one stake. The next spell says whether to bunch or scatter. I read it first and then I tell them.",
      chronomancer: "Two runes, a thread between, and the next spell named in the feed: close, or part. I read the name before the beat, which is the correct beat to read it on. It took me a few tries to remember to.",
      alembist: "Two runes, two elements, a thread between their bearers. The runes alone tell you nothing; read the next spell on his list and it says whether the pair close up or part. Underline the list. Underline it twice.",
      threadweaver: "He stitched two of mine together with a bright thread and a rune each. The next spell on his list says pull it tight or let it out. I read the list before I touch the slack now. His work, my tension.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us wore runes on a bright thread, and the spell named next on his list said what to do: close up, or part. I read it first, as one reads a tide table before launching, and called it, and the pair went the right way.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us carried runes joined by a bright thread. The next spell on his list was named, and the name told the pair whether to stand together or apart. I read it off and called it before they had finished looking up.",
      kenseier: "Two of us carried runes joined by a bright thread, and the name of the spell to come said whether to close or to part. I read his list before moving, once I had learned to look. The order is the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Runes on two of us with a bright thread between. The next spell on his list says close up or spread out. Read it before moving. Once you can read it, it's easy.",
    } },
    "Cinder Rune": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Fire runes. Each bearer carried a ring of flame; the bearers marched to opposite ends, because close together a second fire showed between. The rest of us kept out of both. Dry boots.",
      gloomsworn: "Fire on two of us, a ring round each. They went to opposite ends, and the rest of us kept out of both rings. Stand them close and a second fire blooms between. I know about fires that start in the wrong place.",
      thresher: "Two rings of fire, one on each marked soul. Place them far apart; they flare between if they are close. The rest of us keep out of both. A pyre for two, and no need to invite the neighbours.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring of fire on each of the pair! The rest of us kept out. The two bearers went far apart, because close together a second flame blooms between them and cooks both. I wanted to be one of them. I was.",
      runeguard: "Fire: a ring about each bearer, and the pair must be apart, for a second flame blooms between them if they stand close. It is shown a moment ahead, as a courtesy. I set the bearers at opposite ends of the room and stood outside both.",
      gorgemaw: "A flambe for two, each guest served in a ring of fire. Seat them at opposite ends of the table and leave both plates alone. Seat them close and a second dish flares up between them. Overdone.",
      oathbroker: "Fire attaches to each bearer, a ring apiece. The fine print: the two must remain apart, or a third liability blooms between them. The remaining undersigned stood outside both rings. Countersigned by the survivors.",
      ironpalm: "Fire, one ring on each of the pair. They went to opposite ends. Close together, a third flame grows between. We stood outside both and watched them burn out.",
      ranger: "Ring of fire on each bearer. Send them to opposite ends. Close, and a second flame blooms between. Rest of us outside both rings.",
      shadowblade: "Fire on two of us, a ring round each. The pair parted to opposite ends, as lovers in a tragedy do, and the rest of us kept out of both. Nobody enjoys being close to fire for long. Not I.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of fire on me! And on another poor soul. My element at last, and I loved it, and I went to the far side from my partner, because close together a second flame blooms between us and spoils the show.",
      thermist: "Fire! A ring on each of the pair, hot as I like it. Keep them far apart or a second flame blooms between. The pair separated; the rest of us stayed outside both. I wanted to be a bearer. I was jealous.",
      driftblade: "A ring of fire on each of the pair and they ran to opposite ends of the room, because close together the flame blooms between them, and the rest of us ran wide of both, and it was a good burn to run round.",
      marionettist: "A ring of fire on each of the pair, and a pyrotechnic third if they stand together. They took opposite wings. The rest of the company stayed outside both rings. (A very well-lit second act.)",
      cleric: "A ring of fire on each of the pair, and I asked them, kindly, to part to opposite ends of the room; if they stand close a second fire blooms between them. The rest of us kept out. Burns are hard on young skin.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring of flame on each of the pair, dear. Plant them far apart or a second fire blooms between them. The rest of my lot stood outside both. Fire is only dangerous in the wrong place.",
      chronomancer: "The rings land together and the third flame blooms between them if they stand close, a beat after. The pair went to opposite ends before the beat. I will have been far from my partner already, as I was.",
      alembist: "A ring of fire round each bearer. Keep them well apart and everyone else outside both rings; close bearers brew a second burst between them. Store apart, like anything that goes off.",
      threadweaver: "Fire wants the thread let all the way out: the pair at opposite ends, a ring of flame round each, and the rest of my stitches clear of both. Pulled too short, it caught alight in the middle.",
      tidecaller: "Fire rings on the pair, and fire with a neighbour makes a second fire between. They went to opposite ends of the room, and the rest of us stayed outside both rings. I carried the water out to them.",
      lanternkeeper: "Fire ringed each of the pair, and two fires near one another breed a third between them. So they took opposite ends of the room, and everyone else kept away from both blazes, lanterns included.",
      kenseier: "The fire runes put a ring on each bearer, and a second fire between them if they stood close. So they stood far apart, at opposite ends, and we kept clear of the rings.",
      houndmaster: "Fire runes on two of us. Stood close, a second fire blooms between. So the pair went to opposite ends, and the rest of us stayed clear of both rings. Dogs with the rest.",
    } },
    "Rime Rune": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Frost runes: one blow, in a blue circle halfway between the pair, for all of us to share. The bearers closed up and the line fell in around them. Shoulder to shoulder.",
      gloomsworn: "Frost on a pair of us, and a blue circle halfway between them, one blow to be shared. They closed up, and the rest of us stood in the circle too. Nobody should take the cold alone. I know the cost of that.",
      thresher: "Frost wants the pair brought in together, two guests walked to one table. A blue circle shows halfway between them; they close up, and the rest of us step in under it to share the cold.",
      bloodreaver: "A blue circle between the pair, one great frost for all of us to share! I charged in with the others and let it spread across us. Not enough pain, but plenty of company. Very friendly cold.",
      runeguard: "Frost: the pair bears one shared blow, a blue circle halfway between them, and all of us enter it together, for a blow divided is a blow endured. The sequence is plain. Close up, then all within.",
      gorgemaw: "A cold dish for the whole table, served in a blue ring halfway between the pair. The two closed up, and we all sat down in the ring and took a share. Chilled. Refreshing.",
      oathbroker: "Frost: a single blow, payable in common, in a blue circle halfway between the two bearers. The bearers must close up, and all of the undersigned enter the circle. Debts shared are debts lightened.",
      ironpalm: "Frost on two of us. A blue circle between them. The pair came together. We all stood in the circle and the cold was spoken once, to all of us, and was bearable.",
      ranger: "Frost runes. Blue circle between the pair. They close up. Everyone else in it too. Share the load.",
      shadowblade: "Frost on a pair of us, and a blue circle where they met, for everyone to share. I do not like sharing. I did it anyway, close to the others, and not very gracefully.",
      pyromancer: "Frost! On two of us! The insult! A great blue circle halfway between the pair, for everyone to stand in and share the cold. I stood in it, shivering, planning my revenge on the old man. Hair intact. Pride, less so.",
      thermist: "Frost rune! A blue circle halfway between the pair, one big shared blow. The pair close up and everybody into the circle. Stand in it deliberately, shiver, and swing to the middle of the group.",
      driftblade: "A blue circle opened halfway between the pair and the whole party had to stand in it, which I hate, standing still, but I ran in with everyone and shared the cold and ran right out again as it faded.",
      marionettist: "Frost on a pair of the company, and a blue spotlight between them, one blow for the entire cast. The pair closed up and the company crowded in. (An ensemble piece, for once.)",
      cleric: "Frost on a pair of my flock, and a blue circle halfway between them, one blow for all to share. They closed up and everyone stood in the circle together. A burden shared, my dears, is a burden halved.",
      grovekeeper: "A blue circle between the pair, dear, like a frost pocket in a low field. They closed up and the rest of my lot gathered in and shared it. Frost spread thin does little harm.",
      chronomancer: "A blue circle opens between the pair, a beat before the blow. The pair closed up and we all stood in, in good time. I remember it. I was there already, and so was the cold.",
      alembist: "One heavy cold blow in a blue circle between the bearers. Bearers together, everyone in the circle, the dose divided. Shelve with the warming draughts.",
      threadweaver: "Frost wants the thread pulled tight: the pair walked together, the blue circle sat between them, and I drew every stitch into it so the cold was shared. Pulled it tight and the cold ran off.",
      tidecaller: "Frost on the pair, and a blue circle halfway between them where the blow was to be shared. They closed up and the rest of us came in beside them, all together in the circle, so the cold was divided like a catch.",
      lanternkeeper: "Frost on the pair, and a blue circle halfway between them for the blow to be shared. They closed up, and the rest of us stepped in beside them. Four of us in one circle, and a lantern at its heart.",
      kenseier: "Frost runes, with a blue circle halfway between the pair where one blow would land, to be shared. The bearers came together and the rest of us joined them in it. Divided among us, the cold was small.",
      houndmaster: "Frost runes. Blue circle lands halfway between the pair, and one blow for the lot of us to share. Bearers walked together and we all piled in beside them, hounds included.",
    } },
    "Storm Rune": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lightning runes. A bolt ran from one bearer to the other, burning what lay between. They stood shoulder to shoulder, which made the bolt too short to matter. I kept the rest off the line.",
      gloomsworn: "A bolt runs from one bearer to the other and burns whatever is between. The pair stood shoulder to shoulder so the line was short, and the rest of us kept off it. A short road has little to burn.",
      thresher: "A bolt runs between the two marked, scorching whatever lies in the line, a fuse set alight. If they stand shoulder to shoulder it is too short to catch anything. The rest of us keep off the line.",
      bloodreaver: "A bolt ran from one of the pair to the other and burned whatever stood between them! They stood shoulder to shoulder, which made the bolt a joke, and the rest of us kept clear. Disappointing, and safe.",
      runeguard: "Lightning: a bolt runs from one bearer to the other and burns anything on the line. Bring the bearers close, and the line is too short to harm; all others keep off it. Not hard, merely something one must remember at the time.",
      gorgemaw: "A spiced skewer run from one guest to the other, burning whatever is threaded on it. The pair sat elbow to elbow, so the skewer was a single mouthful, and nobody else was skewered. Tangy.",
      oathbroker: "A bolt of lightning runs between the bearers and burns whoever stands in the line. The fine print is brevity: bearers close, and the line shortens to nothing. All others stay off it. So ordered.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning between two of us, a line from one to the other. They stood close. The line was short. We kept off it.",
      ranger: "Bolt from one bearer to the other. Burns whatever's between. Bearers stand close, line's too short to matter. Others off the line.",
      shadowblade: "A bolt runs from one to the other and burns whatever's between, which is a vulgar way to light a room. The pair stood shoulder to shoulder, and I stayed off the line. Safe, and slightly bored.",
      pyromancer: "Lightning! Bright, loud, and not mine! A bolt ran from one of the pair to the other, burning whatever stood between. They stood shoulder to shoulder so it fizzled, and I kept well off the line. Mostly impressed.",
      thermist: "Bolt! Hot and fast, from one of the pair to the other, burning anything between. Stand them shoulder to shoulder so the line is too short to bite. Stay off it. Fizzled. Disappointing, and thrilling.",
      driftblade: "A bolt ran from one of the pair to the other and burned anything between, so they stood shoulder to shoulder, which for me is torture, and I ran wide of the line as it fizzled out between their elbows.",
      marionettist: "Lightning between two of the company, a line of white fire across the stage. The pair stood elbow to elbow, so the line was too short to matter. The rest of us kept out of the wings. (A sparkling duet.)",
      cleric: "A bolt ran from one bearer to the other and burned whatever stood between. I asked the pair to stand shoulder to shoulder, so it had no room to burn, and everyone else off the line. No one cried out. Good.",
      grovekeeper: "A bolt between the pair, dear, like lightning down a dry fence line. They stood shoulder to shoulder so there was no fence to run along, and the rest of my lot kept off. Short fences don't burn.",
      chronomancer: "The bolt runs from one to the other, a beat after the runes flare. They stood shoulder to shoulder, so it had no distance to run. The rest of us stayed off the line. I will have done this before.",
      alembist: "Note: a bolt running bearer to bearer, burning all between. Bearers side by side, line kept short, everyone else off it. Insulate the shelf. Insulate the alchemist.",
      threadweaver: "Lightning runs the thread itself, so I pulled it tight: the pair side by side, the bolt with nowhere to go, and the rest of my stitches well off the line. A short seam carries no charge.",
      tidecaller: "A bolt ran from one bearer to the other and burned whatever lay between. The pair stood shoulder to shoulder, so it had no road to run, and everyone else kept off the line as I would keep off a rip.",
      lanternkeeper: "Lightning ran along the thread between the pair and scorched whatever stood in its way. They stood close, shoulder to shoulder, leaving the bolt nowhere to go, and the rest of us stayed off the line. I lit nothing near it.",
      kenseier: "Lightning runes, and a bolt between the two bearers that burned whatever stood in its path. Standing side by side gave it no path at all. The rest of us stayed off the line anyway, out of respect.",
      houndmaster: "Lightning runes, a bolt between the bearers burning whatever lies between. Bearers stood close, so the line was short. Rest of us stayed off it anyway. Heel.",
    } },
    "Rift Rune": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Void runes, violet rings, quiet in the middle. The bearers went to opposite sides, since close together the void opens between them. The line kept out of both rings. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The void, which I know, marked a pair of us with violet rings. They went to opposite sides, and the rest of us kept out of both. Bring them close and it opens between them. I would not stand in the middle of that.",
      thresher: "A violet ring round each of the pair, hollow in the middle like a bell without a clapper. Keep them far apart and the rest of us outside both rings; close together, the void opens between them. Empty ground, that.",
      bloodreaver: "A violet ring round each of the pair, and a hole in the middle that eats whatever stands in it! The two went to opposite sides, and the rest of us stayed out of both. I'd have liked to jump in. Rude of me.",
      runeguard: "The void: a violet ring about each bearer with a quiet middle. The bearers part to opposite sides; close together, the void opens between them. I recorded the shape, since voids are seldom so kind as to tell one their size.",
      gorgemaw: "A violet ring with a hollow middle round each of the pair, like a crown of pastry with nothing inside. Keep out of both, and seat the pair far apart, or the void opens between them. Strange. I liked it.",
      oathbroker: "Void: a ring about each bearer, quiet in the middle. The fine print forbids the bearers from standing near each other, or the void opens between them. Remaining parties stayed out of both rings. Clause upheld.",
      ironpalm: "A violet ring on each of the pair, still in the middle. They went to opposite sides. The rest of us stayed out. Close together, they would have opened a hole.",
      ranger: "Void rings on two of us. Violet, quiet inside. Pair to opposite ends. Everyone else outside both rings.",
      shadowblade: "Violet rings on a pair of us, and a void between if they stood close, which is a little too like me. The pair went to opposite sides, the rest of us kept out of both. I know a good hole when I see one.",
      pyromancer: "A violet ring on each of the pair, and a hole in the middle where nothing burns! Unnatural. The two went to opposite sides, and I stayed outside both rings and sulked at the absence of fire.",
      thermist: "Void! Neither hot nor cold, violet, and it eats the middle of a ring. The pair go to opposite sides; close together, the void opens between. The rest of us stayed out. Hard to enjoy something with no temperature.",
      driftblade: "A violet ring on each of the pair, quiet in the middle, and I ran to the far side of the room from my partner so the void wouldn't open between us, and the rest of the party ran wide of both rings.",
      marionettist: "A violet spotlight on each of the pair, hollow at the centre. The two took opposite wings, since close together the void opens between them. The rest of the company stayed outside both. (An empty scene, deliberately.)",
      cleric: "A violet ring around each of the pair, and a quiet hollow in the middle. I asked them to go to opposite sides, for close together the void opens between them. The rest of us kept out of both. They went gently.",
      grovekeeper: "A violet ring on each of the pair, hollow in the middle like a burned-out stump, dear. Far apart, they were fine; close together, the void opened between them. The rest of my lot stayed out of both rings.",
      chronomancer: "The rings land on the pair and the void opens between them a beat later if they stand close. They went to opposite sides before the beat. I will have been far from my partner, as I must.",
      alembist: "Violet ring round each bearer, quiet centre. Everyone else out of the rings, bearers well apart; brought close, they open the void between them. Do not mix. I mixed once. We do not discuss it.",
      threadweaver: "The void wants the thread let out to its full length: the pair at opposite ends, a violet ring round each, my stitches outside both. Pulled too short, the void opened in the middle of the seam.",
      tidecaller: "Violet rings on the pair, hollow in the middle, and close together the void opened between them. They went to opposite sides of the room and the rest of us stayed clear of both rings. Far apart, it was a still pool and no more.",
      lanternkeeper: "Violet rings on the pair, hollow in the middle, and when they stand close the void opens between them. They went to opposite sides and the rest of us stayed clear of both rings. Far apart, the hollow does nothing at all.",
      kenseier: "Void runes: a violet ring on each bearer, hollow in the middle. Close together they opened a void between them. They went to opposite sides and we kept out of the rings. Far apart, it was nothing.",
      houndmaster: "Void runes, a violet ring on each bearer, hollow in the middle. Close together they open a void between. Bearers went to opposite ends and the rest of us kept out of both rings.",
    } },
    "Pyre Rings": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Three rings in the floor. Two burned in turn; the third had no red on it and never caught. I held the line on that one, or on the first once it was ash. Plain ground, best ground.",
      gloomsworn: "Three rings in the floor, two burning in turn, the third left unmarked. I stood on the one with no red, or on the one already burned out. Fire goes out. The dead take comfort in that.",
      thresher: "The floor is laid in three rings, and he burns two of them one after the other. The third is never painted. I stood there, or on the first once it had burned off. A quiet corner of the ballroom.",
      bloodreaver: "Three rings in the floor, and two of them burned one after the other! The third had no red on it. I stood there, or on the one burned out. Safe, boring, and warm. I would have liked a lick of it.",
      runeguard: "Three rings: the middle, the next, the outermost. Two burn, one after the other; the third is never painted, and so is safe, as is the first once spent. A very honest omission, for a sorcerer.",
      gorgemaw: "Three rings on the griddle, and the cook fires two of them in turn. The one with no red is the cool plate. I sat there, or on the first once it had gone cold. Seared.",
      oathbroker: "Three rings of the floor, two struck in sequence, the third left blank. The fine print is the blank: it is exempt. The first ring is also exempt once burned out. We stood there. So recorded.",
      ironpalm: "Three rings. Two burned, one after the other. The third had no red on it. I stood in the one that did not burn, or in the one already spent.",
      ranger: "Three rings. Two burn in turn. Third's unmarked. Stand there, or on the first once it's out.",
      shadowblade: "The floor was split in three rings, two burning in turn, the third left in the dark. I like the dark. I stood on it. Or on the first ring, once it had finished its tantrum.",
      pyromancer: "Rings of fire on the floor! Two of the three burn in turn, gorgeous, but the third has no red on it at all, and that's where I stood, or on the first ring once it had burned out. Lovely fire. Hands off.",
      thermist: "Three rings! Two burn, one after the other, the third is unmarked. Stand on the one with no red, or on the first once it burns out. Do it before the second lights. Singed pride only.",
      driftblade: "Three rings on the floor and two burned in turn, and I ran to the unmarked one, which was the quietest place in the room, or to the first once it had burned out, and the whole time I was moving, never settling.",
      marionettist: "The stage was laid in three rings; two lit in turn, one after the other, and the third left dark. The company took the dark one, or the first ring once its lights had gone out. (Superb lighting design.)",
      cleric: "Three rings in the floor, and two of them burned in turn. The third had no red on it, and I told my flock to stand there, or on the first ring after it had burned out. They found it quickly. Praise be.",
      grovekeeper: "Three rings laid out like beds in a garden, dear. Two burned in turn; the third was left bare. My lot stood on the bare one, or on the first once it was ash. A burned bed is fine, after.",
      chronomancer: "Three rings; the first burns, then the second, and the third is never touched. I will have been on the third already, or on the first after it was ash. It is the order that decides it.",
      alembist: "Three rings of floor, two burning in turn. The ring with no red on it is safe, as is the first ring once spent. Label the rings. Do not label them while burning.",
      threadweaver: "He burned the floor in three hems, two in turn. The one with no red never catches; I gathered every stitch there, or into the first hem once it had burned out. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "Three rings in the floor, two burning in turn. The third wore no red and never caught, so I stood on it, or on the first once it had ebbed to ash. The bare ring was dry ground, and I was glad of it.",
      lanternkeeper: "Of the three rings in the floor, two burned one after the other. The bare one, without red, was the place to be, and so was the first once it had gone to ash. I carried my lantern there and stood in its glow.",
      kenseier: "Three rings were cut in the floor and two of them burned in turn. The third stayed plain, never red. I took that one, or the first after it had gone to ash. Plain ground was the answer, and I was glad of it.",
      houndmaster: "Three rings cut in the floor, two burning in turn. The third stays plain. Stood on that one, or on the first once it burnt out. Whistled the dogs onto plain ground.",
    } },
    "Ice Lances": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lines of ice reached out through some of us and followed. When mine set, I stepped off it; nobody stood in another man's line. Orderly, for once.",
      gloomsworn: "Lines of ice ran out from him through some of us, and followed. When mine set, I stepped off it, and nobody stood in another's. A long straight line is a plain thing. I was grateful for plain.",
      thresher: "Long icicles reach out from him through some of us, following until they set, like lines drawn across a page. When yours sets, step aside. And stay off your neighbour's line.",
      bloodreaver: "Lines of ice drove out through some of us, following us round the room! When mine set I stepped off it, and nobody stood in another's. A near miss, tidy. I could do with a worse one.",
      runeguard: "Lines of ice ran out from him through some of us, following until the last instant. When a line sets, its owner steps off it, and no one stands in another's. Mark each line's owner; it is the only way to read them.",
      gorgemaw: "Long icicles served out from my host through some of the guests, following them round the room. When mine set, I stepped aside, and kept out of the others' portions. Crisp.",
      oathbroker: "A line of ice is drawn through each of several parties, following them until it sets. The fine print: each party steps off their own line, and none steps onto another's. Disputes were short.",
      ironpalm: "Lines of ice came through some of us, following. When mine set, I stepped off it. I did not step into anyone else's.",
      ranger: "Lines of ice through some of us, tracking. When it sets, step off yours. Stay off the others.",
      shadowblade: "Lines of ice drawn through some of us, trailing like a lover's glance. When mine set, I stepped off it, and kept well clear of the others'. I do not share a line.",
      pyromancer: "Lines of ice through some of us! Cold, straight, boring. When mine set, I stepped off, and kept out of everyone else's, and glared at the old man the whole time. Mostly unfrozen, and mostly irritated.",
      thermist: "Lines of ice! Cold, straight, following. Step off when it sets, and never onto another's. I misjudged once, felt the freeze on my sleeve, and swung hot to compensate. Result: a lesson in parallel lines.",
      driftblade: "Lines of ice came out of him through some of us and followed us round the room, and when mine locked I was already off it and across the room, and I never ran into anyone else's.",
      marionettist: "Long lines of ice ran out from our leading man through some of the company, following them across the stage. When each locked, its owner stepped off, and no one crossed another's. (Neat blocking.)",
      cleric: "Lines of ice ran out through some of my flock, following until they set. I called to each: step off your own line when it locks, and never into another's. They did, one by one, like a good choir.",
      grovekeeper: "Lines of ice ran out through some of my lot, dear, following them round like furrows. When yours sets, step aside and stay off the next one over. Cold is a patient plough.",
      chronomancer: "The line follows until the beat it sets. Step off your own on that beat, and not onto another's. I stepped off, and then stepped back, because I forget what I did. Do not step back.",
      alembist: "Lines of ice through several of us, following until they lock. Step out of yours when it sets, never into another's. Keep the shelves out of line, too.",
      threadweaver: "Lines of ice ran through some of my stitches like basting threads, following them. When each locked, its stitch stepped out, and nobody sat on anyone else's line. Basting always comes out.",
      tidecaller: "Lines of ice ran out through some of us and followed, then set. When mine locked I stepped off it, and kept out of everyone else's, the way one keeps out of another boat's wake.",
      lanternkeeper: "Lines of ice ran out through some of us and followed, then set. When mine locked I stepped off it, and kept out of everyone else's. I set my lantern outside every line.",
      kenseier: "Ice ran from some of us in long lines, following, then locked in place. The moment mine locked I walked off it, and I stayed out of everyone else's. Once set, a line is a stroke already finished.",
      houndmaster: "Lines of ice ran out from some of us and chased, then set hard. Soon as mine set I stepped off it and kept clear of the others'. Whistled the hounds off them too.",
    } },
    "Leaping Bolt": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lightning rings jumped from one man to the nearest, a heartbeat apart. We spread wide so it had no near man to jump to, and each walked out of his own ring.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning on one of us, then on the nearest to him, then the next. A heartbeat apart each time. We spread wide so it had nobody near to jump to, and each walked out of their own ring. Loneliness has its uses.",
      thresher: "A ring of lightning on one, then it hops to whoever is nearest, and hops again, a spark through dry paper. Spread wide so it has nobody to hop to, and walk out of your own ring when it comes.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring of lightning on one of us, then on the next nearest, and the next! We spread wide so it had nowhere to jump, and each walked out of the ring when it came. I wanted it to jump to me. It didn't. Pity.",
      runeguard: "A ring of lightning on one of us, then on whoever stands nearest, then the nearest to him; each a heartbeat apart. Keep wide apart, so the jump has no one near; and walk out of your own ring. The order matters.",
      gorgemaw: "A spark passed round the table, from each guest to the nearest. We sat far apart so it had nobody to pass to, and each of us left our seat as it settled. Fizzy.",
      oathbroker: "A ring of lightning, assigned to one party, then transferred to the party nearest, then the next, a heartbeat apart. The fine print: nobody close, no transfer. We spread wide, and each of us quit our ring.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning on one of us. Then the nearest. Then the nearest to that one. We stood far apart so it had no one to reach, and each walked out of the ring.",
      ranger: "Lightning ring on one, jumps to the nearest. Heartbeat apart. Spread wide, nobody close. Walk out of your ring.",
      shadowblade: "A ring of lightning on one of us, then the nearest, then the next. Everyone kept their distance, which comes naturally to me, and each walked out of their own ring. Nobody to jump to, nobody to blame.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of lightning on one of us, then the nearest, and the next! A spark gone shopping. We spread wide so it had nobody to jump to, and I walked out of my ring with my hair standing on end. Mostly unfazed.",
      thermist: "Bolt! Ring of lightning on one of us, jumping to the nearest, and the next. Spread wide so nobody is near; walk out of your own ring when it settles. Hair standing straight. Electrified. Delighted.",
      driftblade: "Lightning on one of us, then the nearest, then the next, a heartbeat apart, so we ran far from each other to give it nothing to jump to, and each of us ran out of our own ring and kept running.",
      marionettist: "A ring of lightning on one of the company, then the nearest, then the nearest again, a heartbeat apart. The company spread to the far corners so it had nowhere to jump. (Stage left, stage right, exit all.)",
      cleric: "A ring of lightning fell on one of my flock, then jumped to whoever stood nearest. I asked them to spread wide, and to walk out of their ring as it landed. They scattered like sparrows and nobody was burned.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring of lightning on one, then the nearest, then the next, dear, like a spark through dry grass. My lot spread wide so it had nowhere to go, and each walked out of their own ring.",
      chronomancer: "A ring of lightning on one, then on the nearest a heartbeat later, then the next. We spread wide beforehand, and each walked out of their ring on the beat. I will have spread wide earlier next time.",
      alembist: "Lightning on one, jumping to the nearest, a heartbeat apart. Everyone well apart, and walk out of your own ring. Store reactive flasks separately, preferably in another county.",
      threadweaver: "The bolt ran from one stitch to the nearest like a needle down a hem. I let the slack out so nobody was close, and each walked out of their own ring. A loose hem carries nothing.",
      tidecaller: "A ring of lightning fell on one of us, then leapt to whoever stood nearest, a heartbeat apart. We kept far apart so it had no near one to leap to, and each walked out of their own ring as it landed.",
      lanternkeeper: "Lightning fell in a ring on one of us, then leapt to whoever stood nearest. So nobody stood near anybody: we spread wide, and each walked out of their own ring as it landed. Scattered people are poorly served by a lantern. I trimmed the wicks and waited.",
      kenseier: "Lightning ringed one of us and hunted the nearest body, a heartbeat later. Every one of us stood apart, so it found nothing to hunt, and each stepped out of their own ring. Distance is its own kind of guard.",
      houndmaster: "Lightning ringed one of us, then jumped to the nearest body. Everyone stood wide, so it had nothing to find. Stepped out of my own ring. Kept the dogs apart too.",
    } },
    "Void Rifts": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Rifts opened under each of us where we stood, then again where we had walked. I moved off my ground and then moved again. Standing firm is for walls. This was for boots.",
      gloomsworn: "A rift opened under each of us, then again where we had gone. I stepped off, then stepped again. A man who has been put in the ground once is in no hurry to be put there twice.",
      thresher: "Holes open under each of us, then again wherever we have moved to. Leave the first, then leave the second. The floor is no use if it has gone from under you. I decline to stand there.",
      bloodreaver: "Rifts under every one of us, then again where we'd walked to! I moved off my spot, then moved again, feeling the edge go by like a sword. Not a scratch. A shame. Close, and close is fun.",
      runeguard: "Rifts open beneath each of us as we stand, and again where we moved to. Leave the first place; leave the second. It is the only floor-reading in the tower that rewards inattention to one's own feet.",
      gorgemaw: "The floor opened under my chair, then again where I had moved it. Leave the first seat, then leave the second. Hollow, like a souffle gone wrong.",
      oathbroker: "A rift is opened beneath each party where they stand, and again where they moved. The fine print is the second opening: vacate the first place, then the second. We forfeited both seats.",
      ironpalm: "The floor opened where I stood. I moved. It opened again where I had moved. I moved again. Stillness was not the lesson.",
      ranger: "Rifts under each of us. Move. Another where you went. Move again. Don't stop.",
      shadowblade: "Rifts opened under each of us, then again where we'd gone. I stepped off both, which was elegant, I think, if anyone noticed. A hole in the floor is a rude place to be seen.",
      pyromancer: "Holes opened under me, then again where I'd moved to! I loathe being chased off a perfectly good spot, but I did, twice, without dropping a flame. Not even a singe. Frankly I am a little proud.",
      thermist: "Rifts! Under each of us, then again where we moved. Leave your spot, then leave the new one too. I ran, I jumped, I laughed. Void is neither hot nor cold. Hard to enjoy.",
      driftblade: "A rift opened under each of us where we stood and again where we had run to, so I never stopped and I never stood and I kept running through, round, and out of every hole he opened, and it was perfect.",
      marionettist: "Trapdoors opened under each of the company where they stood, and again where they had moved to. Exit the first, exit the second. (The Doll took a different aisle entirely.)",
      cleric: "The floor opened under each of my flock where they stood, and again where they had gone. I called out: leave your spot, then leave the next. They did, and bless them, they kept moving.",
      grovekeeper: "Holes opened under each of us, dear, and again where we'd moved, like a mole in a bed. Leave the first place, then leave the second. A garden is no place to stand still.",
      chronomancer: "The rift opens beneath, then again where they went. Leave on the beat, and leave again on the next. I will have moved twice. It was exactly twice. I counted. It was three.",
      alembist: "Rifts beneath each of us, then again where we moved. Leave your spot, then leave the new one. Do not set a flask down. I set a flask down. It is in the void now, with the others.",
      threadweaver: "The void opened under every stitch, then again where they had moved. Keep them walking, off the first and off the second. A needle that stands still gets pricked.",
      tidecaller: "The floor opened under each of us where we stood, and again where we had walked. I left my place, and then the next one, and kept moving like a tide that does not stay. Standing still was the only mistake.",
      lanternkeeper: "The floor opened under each of us where we stood, and again where we had walked. Standing still was the only mistake. I picked my lantern up and kept moving with it; a lamp left behind is a lamp lost.",
      kenseier: "Rifts opened under each of us where we stood, and again where we had walked. I left my ground, and then the next. Stillness has its place, and this was not it.",
      houndmaster: "Rifts opened under each of us where we stood, and again where we'd walked. Left my ground and then the next. Don't stop. Dogs keep moving too, heel.",
    } },
    "The Binding": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four sigils woke and the elementals rose from the middle, slow, each following me. I marched each to the sigil of its own colour and held it there. The line kept on the Archmage and clear of the falling light.",
      gloomsworn: "Four sigils woke, and elementals rose from the middle to follow me, slow as the tide. I led each to the sigil of its own colour and held it there. The rest kept their blades in him. A strange watch, with company.",
      thresher: "Four sigils, four spirits, and each follows our knight like a shadow. Walk it into the sigil of its own colour and hold it there a moment. One left loose blasts the room. The rest of us keep reaping him.",
      bloodreaver: "Elementals rose and followed me, and I led each into the sigil of its own colour and held it! Slow work, and I wanted to hit them. The others struck the old man and kept clear of the falling light. Hard on my temper.",
      runeguard: "Four sigils woke and elementals rose from the middle, each following the shield. I walked each into the sigil of its own colour and held it there. A great rite, correct in all its parts. One left unbound blasts the room.",
      gorgemaw: "Four sigils, four plates, each elemental walked to the plate of its own colour by our shield and held there a moment. Unbound, one spoils the whole meal. I kept eating at my host, off the falling light.",
      oathbroker: "Each elemental is a debt carried by the shield, and must be settled at its own sigil: the matching colour, held a moment. One left unbound defaults, and the whole estate pays. The rest of us struck him, clear of the falling light.",
      ironpalm: "Four elementals rose and followed me, slowly. I walked each to the sigil of its own colour and held it there. The others struck the Archmage and kept off the falling light.",
      ranger: "Four sigils woke. Elementals follow the shield. Lead each into the sigil of its own colour, hold a moment. One left loose blasts the room. Others keep shooting him, off the falling light.",
      shadowblade: "The sigils woke and elementals followed our shield like children after a piper. Each had to be walked into the sigil of its colour and held. Meanwhile the rest of us struck the old man from the side, off the falling light.",
      pyromancer: "Elementals rose from the floor, one at a time, and followed our knight like stray cats! Each had to be walked into the sigil of its own colour, even my fire one. We hit the Archmage and stayed off the falling light. A show-off's paradise.",
      thermist: "Four sigils, four elementals, one at a time, following the shield! Each into the sigil of its own colour. Strike him while they walk, off the falling light. One unbound blasts the room. Hot, cold, bolt, void: I wept.",
      driftblade: "Elementals rose and followed our knight slowly, like fog, and he walked each into the sigil of its own colour and held it, and the rest of us ran in circles round the old man striking him and slipping the falling light.",
      marionettist: "The sigils woke and four elementals rose, each following the lead player, who walked each to the lamp of its own colour. The rest of the company struck the star and kept out of the falling light. (Splendid pageantry.)",
      cleric: "Elementals rose from the floor, each following our knight, slow and patient. He walked each into the sigil of its own colour and held it there. I kept him mended, and the rest of us struck at the old man, off the falling light.",
      grovekeeper: "Four sigils woke, dear, and four elementals rose and followed our knight like ducklings. Each into the sigil of its own colour, held a moment. One left loose blasts the room. The rest of us struck the old man, off the falling light.",
      chronomancer: "The sigils woke, and the elementals follow the shield in order, one at a time. Each must be led to the sigil of its own colour and held. I will have struck him a hundred times in that interval. It felt like it.",
      alembist: "Four sigils, elementals following our shield. Each led into the sigil of its own colour and held; the rest keep striking him, off the falling light. Label by colour. Do not put the red one near the blue.",
      threadweaver: "Our knight threaded each elemental behind him to the sigil of its own colour and held it there. One left loose tears the whole hem. The rest of us kept stitching at him, off the falling light.",
      tidecaller: "Four sigils woke and the elementals rose from the middle, slow, following our knight. He led each to the sigil of its own colour and held it there while I kept his water high, and the rest of us struck at the Archmage, out of the falling light.",
      lanternkeeper: "Elementals climbed out of four sigils and plodded after our knight. He walked each to the sigil that matched it and kept it there, and I kept a lantern hung on him the whole while. The rest of us went for the Archmage, away from the falling light.",
      kenseier: "Four sigils woke and four elementals climbed out of the middle, slowly, all after me. I led each to the sigil that matched its colour and held it there, and the others struck at the Archmage, out of the falling light. A long walk, and every step counted.",
      houndmaster: "Four sigils woke and four elementals climbed out, after the shield-man. He led each to the sigil of its own colour and held it. The rest of us struck the wizard, off the falling light. Kept the dogs off the elementals.",
    } },
    "Call of the Circle": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He called one elemental at a time, its colour naming its sigil. I walked it there; it followed; the line made room. Slow march, orderly.",
      gloomsworn: "One elemental at a time, and its colour named the sigil. I led it there, slowly, and the others made room for the procession. I have escorted worse company.",
      thresher: "One spirit at a time, and the colour says which sigil it belongs in. Our knight walks it there, and the rest of us make room. A guest escorted to the right door through a crowded hall.",
      bloodreaver: "One elemental at a time, and its colour says where it goes! The shield walks it there, and I gave him room, which is hard for me. I wanted to hit it. I hit the old man instead.",
      runeguard: "He calls them one at a time; its colour names its sigil. The shield walks it there, and the rest give room. A tidy, almost courtly arrangement, and as foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host sent out one dish at a time, each coloured for its own plate. Our shield carried it there, and the rest of us made way for the waiter. Hot, then cold.",
      oathbroker: "One elemental per summons, and the colour names the sigil where it must be settled. The shield escorts it; the rest give room. A tidy order of business.",
      ironpalm: "One at a time. The colour named the sigil. The shield walked it there. We made room.",
      ranger: "One elemental at a time. Colour says which sigil. Shield walks it. Everyone else clear of the path.",
      shadowblade: "He summoned them one at a time, each coloured for its place. Our knight walked each to its sigil while the rest of us gave room, like a procession I wasn't invited to. I preferred it that way.",
      pyromancer: "One elemental at a time, and the colour tells the sigil! Fire's obviously the best-looking. The shield walks each one there, and the rest of us give room, and I miss being chased, honestly.",
      thermist: "Elementals! One at a time, coloured by element, sigil to match. The shield leads it there; the rest of us give room. Hot, cold, bolt, void, in order. I could watch it forever. I did.",
      driftblade: "One elemental at a time and each one coloured for its sigil, and our knight walked it there slow as a snail and the rest of us gave him lanes, and I ran circles round the old man, still striking.",
      marionettist: "He called them out one at a time, each costumed in the colour of its sigil, and the lead player walked it to its mark while the company stepped aside. (The Doll waved to them all.)",
      cleric: "One elemental at a time, and its colour told our knight where to lead it. The rest of us gave him room, and I kept him mended on the walk. Not a thing to hurry.",
      grovekeeper: "One at a time, dear, each coloured to its own sigil, like seedlings to their beds. Our knight led each there while the rest of us gave room. A patient job, and I do admire patience.",
      chronomancer: "One elemental at a time, colour first, then sigil. The shield leads it there; we give room, in order. I have seen this walk before. I think I will see it again.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one elemental per call. Remedy: its colour names its sigil; our shield walks it there, the rest give room. Label by colour, as always. Do not put the red one near the blue.",
      threadweaver: "He called one at a time, and the colour said which sigil it was sewn to. Our knight walked it there and the rest of my stitches kept clear of the seam. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "One at a time he called them, and the colour of each named its sigil. Our knight walked it there and the rest of us made room along the way. I went beside him with a little water for the road. No hurry.",
      lanternkeeper: "He summoned them singly, and each elemental's colour named the sigil it belonged to. Our knight led it across, the rest of us cleared his road, and I walked beside him with a lantern. Not a thing to hurry.",
      kenseier: "He called them one at a time, the colour naming its sigil. I walked each there, it followed, and the others made room. No hurry, but no pause either.",
      houndmaster: "He called the elementals one at a time, the colour naming the sigil. The shield-man walks each there. Everyone else cleared the path. No hurry, no pause.",
    } },
    "Convergence": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Every sigil lit at once, each its own colour, and worse where two met. The clear floor was out by the wall between the sigils. I took the line there, clear of the overlaps. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Every sigil lit together, each in its colour, and worse where two overlapped. The clear ground was by the wall, between the sigils. I took the watch out there. It was quiet, as the edges of things are.",
      thresher: "All four sigils flare at once, and where two circles overlap the ground goes bad twice over. The clear ground lies out by the wall, between them. A rush for the exits. Everybody to the wall.",
      bloodreaver: "All four sigils lit at once, and where two circles overlapped it hurt twice! I wanted a taste, but I took the others out to the clear ground by the wall between the sigils. It's no fun being sensible.",
      runeguard: "All four sigils lit together, each circle its own element, and where two overlapped a reaction filled the lens between. The floor out by the wall, between the sigils, was clear. I wrote the geometry in the margin and drew a small star beside it.",
      gorgemaw: "Every burner lit at once, and where two meet, the flavours fight. I took my plate to the edge of the table, by the wall between the sigils, clear of the clashes. Too much at once.",
      oathbroker: "The final audit: all four circles lit, and where two overlap a reaction fills the lens. The fine print is the clear floor by the wall between the sigils. The undersigned stood there. Account closed.",
      ironpalm: "All four lit at once. Where two overlapped, it grew worse. The clear ground was by the wall, between the sigils. I stood there, and we all breathed.",
      ranger: "All four sigils lit. Overlaps worse. Clear ground out by the wall, between the sigils. Everyone there.",
      shadowblade: "All four sigils lit together, which was vulgar, and where two met it was worse. The clear ground was out by the wall, between the circles, in the dark. I took everyone there. I always knew the edges were mine.",
      pyromancer: "Every sigil lit at once, a glorious light show, and where two circles overlapped something exploded! The clear ground was out by the wall, between the sigils. I took my sleeves there at a run.",
      thermist: "Convergence! All four elements at once, and overlaps where they react! The clear floor is out by the wall, between the sigils. Run there. Run there now. I did. Smoke from my sleeve the whole way.",
      driftblade: "All four sigils lit up together and where they overlapped it was bad, and I ran out between them to the wall where the floor was clear, through the gap, along the edge, and nowhere else.",
      marionettist: "All four lamps lit at once, a blaze of colour, and where two overlapped, a reaction flared between them. The company took the dark wings between the sigils, by the wall. (Grand finale, safely staged.)",
      cleric: "Every sigil lit at once, each in its own colour, and worse where two met. I called my flock out to the clear floor by the wall, between the sigils. They went. It was quiet there, and I was grateful.",
      grovekeeper: "All four sigils lit at once, dear, like four fires in one field, and worse where they touched. The clear ground was out by the wall, between them. My lot gathered there, and breathed.",
      chronomancer: "All four sigils light together, and where two meet the floor reacts a beat later. The clear ground is by the wall, between them. I will have stood there already. I had; I was early for once.",
      alembist: "Every sigil lit at once, overlaps worse. The clear ground is by the wall between the sigils, away from where two circles meet. Do not mix reagents. Do not mix anything.",
      threadweaver: "Every circle lit at once, and the overlaps were where the seams pucker. I took my stitches to the hem by the wall, between the sigils, clear of the overlaps. Pucker means pain.",
      tidecaller: "Every sigil lit at once, and worse where two met. The clear floor lay out by the wall, between the sigils, and we gathered there, a quiet cove in a loud bay. I had the water high before we went.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every sigil lit at once, and worse where two met. The clear floor lay out by the wall, between the sigils, and we gathered there. I set a lantern in that quiet corner before they lit, so we had a light to go to.",
      kenseier: "Every sigil lit together, brighter where two overlapped. The clear floor was out by the wall, between the sigils, so we went there and waited. A quiet gap between loud notes, and the only place to stand.",
      houndmaster: "Every sigil lit at once, worse where two overlap. Clear ground out by the wall, between the sigils. Whole pack went there and waited. A gap between loud notes.",
    } },
    "Counterspell": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He cast it again, flung to the far side of the room, the copy drawn on the floor at once. Ground that had been clear could be hit. I looked across the room, not at my boots, and moved. Nobody stays on ground he trusts.",
      gloomsworn: "He threw the last spell again, to the far side of the room, drawn on the floor at once. Ground that was safe was not. I looked across the room, not at my feet, and moved. The dead learn to look behind them.",
      thresher: "He throws the last pattern again, clean across the room, the copy showing at once. Ground that was clear is cut up like a shattered mirror. Look across, not down, and go.",
      bloodreaver: "He threw the last spell again to the far side of the room, and it showed on the floor at once! Ground that was clear was suddenly hot. I looked across the room and moved. Twice the fun for half the reading.",
      runeguard: "He cast the last spell a second time, flung through the middle of the room to the far side, and the copy is drawn at once on the floor. Look across the room, not where you stand. I recorded the second casting, and the first, and the trap between them.",
      gorgemaw: "My host served the last course again, flipped through the middle of the table to the far side. The copy shows on the cloth at once. I moved my plate. Reheated.",
      oathbroker: "A rider on the last spell: it is issued again, mirrored through the middle of the room, and drawn at once. Fine print: ground once clear may now be hit. Inspect the far side, and relocate.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the last word again, thrown to the far side of the room. The floor showed it at once. I looked across the room, and moved.",
      ranger: "Last spell again, thrown across the room. Copy shows on the floor at once. Look across. Move.",
      shadowblade: "He cast it again, mirrored to the far side of the room, and the floor drew it at once. Ground that was safe was not. I looked across the room, not down, and slipped away before the copy landed.",
      pyromancer: "He cast the last spell again, thrown to the far side, and the copy showed up on the floor right away! Ground I'd just trusted was suddenly on fire. I looked across the room and moved. Imitation, how flattering!",
      thermist: "Counterspell! The last pattern again, mirrored to the far side, the copy drawn at once. Look across the room. Move! I looked at my feet once. Cinder. Lesson learned.",
      driftblade: "He threw the last spell again, to the far side, mirrored, and the copy showed up on the floor at once, so I looked across the room and ran, through the middle, to the opposite side and out of it.",
      marionettist: "The star repeated the last scene, thrown to the far wing, the copy drawn on the stage at once. Ground that was clear was clear no longer. The company looked across the stage and moved. (An unwelcome encore.)",
      cleric: "He threw the last spell again to the far side of the room, and the floor showed it at once. Ground that was clear was clear no longer. I called to my flock: look across the room, not at your feet.",
      grovekeeper: "He casts the last pattern again, dear, thrown through the middle to the far side, the copy drawn at once. Ground that was clear is suddenly fire. Look across the room and go. A second frost kills what the first spared.",
      chronomancer: "The last spell again, thrown to the far side a few moments after the first, the copy drawn at once. I looked across the room and moved. I have done this before. I have not enjoyed it before, either.",
      alembist: "Second dose: the last spell's areas again, mirrored through the middle. Read the copy at once, look across the room, move. I would have liked to bottle it.",
      threadweaver: "He turned the last pattern inside out, through the middle, to the far side. The copy shows at once. I looked across the whole hem and moved my stitches. A pattern is no good once it has been repeated.",
      tidecaller: "He cast the last spell again, thrown across the room, and the floor showed it at once. Ground that had been calm was a rip now. I looked across the whole room, not down at my feet, and moved before the water turned.",
      lanternkeeper: "He threw the last spell again to the far side of the room, and the floor showed it at once. Ground that was clear was clear no longer. I looked across the whole room, not at my feet, and moved my lanterns before the floor did.",
      kenseier: "He cast the last spell again, flung across the room, its copy drawn on the floor at once. Ground that had been clear could be hit. I looked across the whole room, not at my feet, and moved. Never trust a quiet place.",
      houndmaster: "He threw the last spell again across the room, its copy drawn on the floor at once. Ground that was clear can be hit now. Looked across the room, not at my boots, and moved. Dogs too.",
    } },
    "Rune Swap": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The runes jumped to two other men while we watched. The coming spell had not changed, so the new bearers closed ranks or kept apart as it asked. I stopped trusting names, and watched the runes.",
      gloomsworn: "The runes lifted off the first pair and landed on two others. The coming spell had not changed, so the new bearers did as it asked. The watch had to learn to read the second heads.",
      thresher: "The runes lift off the first pair and land on two others, a pair of masks passed along. The spell to come is the same, so the new pair closes or parts as before. Follow the rune, not the face.",
      bloodreaver: "The runes jumped off me! To two other heads! The coming spell hadn't changed, so whoever carried them now closed up or kept apart as it asked. I felt cheated of my part in it. Fair play.",
      runeguard: "After the Mark, the runes leapt to two other heroes, and the spell to come was unchanged. The new bearers close or part according to its element. I now read the head, not the tag. Well. I always read the tag too.",
      gorgemaw: "My host moved the place cards: the runes jumped to two other guests. The next dish had not changed, so the new pair sat close or apart as it required. A rearrangement, not a new course.",
      oathbroker: "A sudden assignment: the runes transferred to two other parties, though the spell on the docket had not changed. The new bearers owe the same terms as the old. An assignment does not alter the obligation.",
      ironpalm: "The runes moved to two others. The word was the same. The new pair listened and did as it asked.",
      ranger: "Runes jumped to two others. Spell's the same. New pair does what it says. Watch the heads, not the runes.",
      shadowblade: "The runes slipped off the first pair and landed on two others, as if they had somewhere better to be. The next spell had not changed, so the new bearers closed or parted as it said. A little sleight of hand, but I saw it.",
      pyromancer: "The runes jumped to two other heads while I was still admiring mine! The coming spell hadn't changed, so whoever had them now closed up or fanned out accordingly. Unfair, and a little thrilling.",
      thermist: "Runes jumped! Off the pair, onto two others, mid-experiment. The spell hasn't changed, so the new bearers close or part as the element says. Track the runes, not the faces. Fast.",
      driftblade: "The runes jumped off the first two and landed on two others, and the coming spell hadn't changed, so the new pair ran together or apart as it asked, and I ran with whoever carried them, whoever they were.",
      marionettist: "After the first cue, the runes passed to two other players, though the scene had not changed. The new pair closed or parted as the element required. (A recast mid-act. Scandalous, and effective.)",
      cleric: "The runes lifted from the pair and settled on two others. The spell to come had not changed, so the new pair closed or parted as it told them. I called it out quickly, and gently. They moved at once.",
      grovekeeper: "The runes lifted off the first pair, dear, and landed on two others, like seed blown to a new bed. The coming spell had not changed, so the new pair closed or parted just the same.",
      chronomancer: "The runes jump to two other heads a beat after they land. The coming spell is the same, so the new bearers close or part as it says. I will have kept my eyes on the thread, not the face. Did.",
      alembist: "Runes jump to two others. The coming spell is unchanged; the new bearers close or part as its element requires. Relabel the bearers. Quickly, with a steady hand.",
      threadweaver: "He unpicked the thread and sewed it onto two other stitches. The coming spell had not changed, so the new pair pulled tight or let out as it asked. A good tailor undoes a seam without a trace. He is a good tailor.",
      tidecaller: "The runes slipped from the pair and settled on two others, as a rip moves a sandbar. The coming spell had not changed, so the new pair closed or parted just as it told them. I stopped watching names and watched who wore them.",
      lanternkeeper: "The runes jumped to two other people. What the next spell asked of the pair had not changed, so the new bearers did it, together or apart. I quit watching the names and began watching the runes.",
      kenseier: "The runes slid off the two bearers onto two others while we watched. The spell that was coming had not changed, so the new pair closed or parted exactly as it told them. Names mean nothing; I followed the runes.",
      houndmaster: "Runes jumped to two others while we watched. Same spell coming, so the new pair did what it said. Watched the heads, not the runes. Whistled the dogs clear of the new pair.",
    } },
    "Arcane Mark": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood, drawn at once. The whole line walked into it, the marked man included, and we shared the blow.",
      gloomsworn: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood, shown at once. We all walked into it, its owner too, and shared the blow. A weight carried by many is a lighter weight. I had never tried it.",
      thresher: "A big blue ring springs up where one of us was standing, like a bloom of pale fire. All of us step into it, its owner too, and share out the blow. Wrong to leave a companion holding the whole of it.",
      bloodreaver: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood, and we all charged in and shared the blow! The owner too. It was a gentle one, split so many ways. I wanted a bigger piece. Selfish, I know.",
      runeguard: "A blue ring rose on the spot where one of us had been, drawn at once. We all entered it, its owner among us, and divided the blow. His one honest piece of arithmetic. The sum divides.",
      gorgemaw: "A blue plate set where one guest had been sitting. We all came to it, the guest too, and shared it out. Cool, and filling.",
      oathbroker: "A claim is entered against the spot where one of us had stood, in a blue ring. The undersigned all enter it, the owner included, and divide the liability. A shared debt is easily paid.",
      ironpalm: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood. We all stepped into it, the owner too. One blow, shared by all, and it was light.",
      ranger: "Blue ring where one of us was standing. Everyone in, owner too. Split the blow. Done.",
      shadowblade: "A blue ring opened up where one of us had been, an unwelcome spotlight. We all stepped into it together, which I found distasteful, and the blow was divided into nothing. Grudgingly effective.",
      pyromancer: "A great blue ring rose up where one of us had been standing, drawn at once! Everyone stepped into it, the owner too, and shared the blow. I'd have preferred a fire. But shared is lighter, apparently.",
      thermist: "Mark! A blue ring where one of us had stood, one big shared blow. Everyone in, the owner included. Gone in a flash. Result: dividing the dose divides the damage. Obvious. Marvellous.",
      driftblade: "A blue ring opened where one of us had stood and I ran into it with the rest, the owner too, the opposite of what I like, and shared the blow, and was out of it the instant it was done.",
      marionettist: "A blue spotlight fell on the spot where one of the company had stood. The entire cast stepped into it, the owner too, and shared the blow. (An ensemble piece, for once. Splendid.)",
      cleric: "A blue ring rose where one of my flock had been standing, shown at once. I called everyone into it, the owner too, and the blow was divided among us. A shared sorrow is always kinder.",
      grovekeeper: "A blue ring rose up where one of my lot had been standing, dear, quick as a toadstool. We all stepped in, the owner too, and shared the blow among us. Many hands, light work.",
      chronomancer: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood, a beat after they left it. We all stepped in, the owner too, and divided the blow. I'd already been standing in it. I think. It was simply the moment.",
      alembist: "A blue ring on the spot where one of us stood. Everyone in, owner included, the dose divided. Label the old spot. Do not drop anything there.",
      threadweaver: "A blue ring came up where one of my stitches had been, and I drew every thread back into it, its owner too, and we shared the blow. A heavy cloth is carried by many hands.",
      tidecaller: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood. We all walked into it, its owner too, and shared the blow, which is how a heavy wave is made small. I raised the water as we went in.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood. All of us walked into it, its owner too, to share the blow. I set a lantern in the middle, and we stood four in one glow.",
      kenseier: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood, drawn at once. We all walked into it, the marked one with us, and shared the blow. Many hands on one hilt. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "A blue ring rose where one of us had stood. All of us walked into it, the marked one with us, and split the blow. Called the dogs in as well. Many hands, light load.",
    } },
  },
};
